  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation is on the explicitly declared function `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Contents]/fn[format_node_content]`. It is a fenced commentary citation, so the justification is tracked, as the built-in requires.\n\n- **Substantive reason:** The commentary says this is a storage-only domain that verifies output decoding without reading or presenting node contents. It says a formatter call would be an unwanted graph access. It says agent fixtures exercise formatting separately. This is a design reason, not just difficulty.\n- **Agrees with the source:** The function body is `panic!(\"storage operations must not format graph contents\")`, a deliberate guard that fails if it runs. `Contents` requires the method, so it must exist even though it should never run. The only test using `StoredGraph` is `malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors`. It exercises `verify`, `store_evidence`, `load_evidence`, and output decoding. It never formats or reads node contents.\n- **Formatting is covered elsewhere:** Other fixtures do exercise formatting. `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/contracts.rs` calls `format_node_content` in `identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot`. The agent fixtures in `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs` implement it for real. This supports the claim that formatting is covered elsewhere.\n- **Scope:** The citation is a function-level exception for a function that is expected never to run. That matches the built-in's allowance for an explicit function with a body.\n\nI found no gap.",
